I was thinking of adding a check near the end of `BaseTuner.inject_adapter`, after the modules have been replaced. It would check the actual adapter layers rather than the configured target names:

```python
affected = []
if torch.backends.mha.get_fastpath_enabled():
    for name, layer in model.named_modules():
        if type(layer) is not nn.TransformerEncoderLayer or not layer.self_attn.batch_first:
            continue

        for slot in ("linear1", "linear2", "self_attn", "norm1", "norm2", "self_attn.out_proj"):
            module = layer.get_submodule(slot)
            if isinstance(module, BaseTunerLayer) and adapter_name in module._get_available_adapters():
                affected.append(f"{name}.{slot}".lstrip("."))
```

PEFT could then show a single warning if any affected modules are found. However, I tested this and I agree with your concern. It would still warn in cases where the fast path cannot run, or when the model is only used for training.  So I think documenting
